# Building the native x86-64/Linux compiler

The native x86-64/LP64 target is the only maintained configuration. To build
and install it directly on a Debian-compatible x86-64 Linux host, install the
build dependencies first. For Debian and Ubuntu, use:

```sh
sudo apt-get install \
    binutils bison diffutils file flex gcc libc6-dev make patch perl \
    xutils-dev
```

Configure out of tree, create the installation directories expected by the
historical build system, and build with the supplied GCC wrapper:

```sh
source_dir=$PWD
build_dir=/tmp/tickc-build
prefix=/tmp/tickc-install

mkdir -p "$build_dir" "$prefix/build" "$prefix/bin" "$prefix/lib"
cd "$build_dir"

"$source_dir/configure" \
    --build=x86_64-pc-linux-gnu \
    --host=x86_64-pc-linux-gnu \
    --target=x86_64-pc-linux-gnu \
    --prefix="$prefix"

make MODE=debug CC="$source_dir/env/linux-x86_64/scripts/gcc-wrapper.sh"
```

Run these commands from the repository root so that `source_dir` identifies
the source tree. The installed compiler is `$prefix/bin/tcc` (with the values
above, `/tmp/tickc-install/bin/tcc`). Use `MODE=optimize` for an optimized
build.

The wrapper selects GNU C89 for historical configure checks and source files.
Passing `CC=gcc` directly can fail with current GCC releases because those
checks contain implicit `int` declarations that newer default language modes
reject.

The former i386/ILP32 target is archived at merge commit `d6d848d`, the final
revision where its complete suite passed alongside x86-64. Its backend,
container, CI workflow, and target-specific tests are intentionally absent
from the maintained tree. See `doc/modernization/i386-retirement.md`.

The removed SPARC, MIPS, SStrix, Alpha, DOS, and OpenBSD material remains
available in Git history. The last merged SStrix baseline is commit
`edcd4d1`.
